WEBVTT

00:00:00.568 --> 00:00:26.480
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. It's Friday, and that means Wild Spaces and Outdoor Life. Today we've got Williams Lake's outdoor summer shows, down to 219 people a night, and the Hell Raving Creek planting, where endangered whitebark pine is going back into burned Chilcotin high country by helicopter. Plus Dwayne Davis, painting holiday windows since 1983. Our deep dive asks whether staying put is how burned country comes back. Stay with us.

00:00:39.955 --> 00:00:58.251
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Friday, November 27th, 2026, and today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:59.251 --> 00:01:36.561
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we go past the last fence line, where crews plant, sensors listen, and people who live out there decide how long to stay. Quick weather check. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's mainly clear and around 1 degree, with a high of 3 and a low of minus 1 today, plus slight snow showers, a 59 percent chance of precipitation, about 2 millimetres. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around minus 2 and mainly clear, Williams Lake is around minus 1 and mainly clear, Quesnel is around 2 and overcast, and Alexis Creek is around minus 1 and mainly clear.

00:01:36.924 --> 00:01:38.116
<v Casey>Stay with us for the news.

00:01:39.516 --> 00:02:20.850
<v Casey>Most of today's news is close to home, and a lot of it is about the weather, the water, and the people who keep showing up for both. Performances in the Park at Boitanio Park in Williams Lake drew an average of 219 people an evening this summer, down from 237 the year before. The Central Cariboo Arts and Culture Society's executive director, Melissa Normandin, told a November 25th Central Cariboo Joint Committee meeting that the weather is to blame. The free series ran July 9th to August 27th, with 16 acts, seven of them local, on a budget of $36,155. Williams Lake First Nation became a core funding partner in June, alongside the City of Williams Lake and the Cariboo Regional District.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.020
<v Riley>Eighteen fewer people a night. Outdoor shows always come down to the forecast.

00:02:27.662 --> 00:02:51.806
<v Riley>Downtown Williams Lake is dressing for winter. Dwayne Davis painted his first holiday shop window in 1983, at Shields Brake and Muffler, and he's back at it this year on storefronts including Paradise Cinemas, Subway, Panago Pizza, and Denny's. City staff have put up Christmas lights around town. Davis says he doesn't make much from the windows. The comments from passersby are the payment.

00:02:52.256 --> 00:02:54.640
<v Casey>Forty-some years of windows, paid in comments.

00:02:56.465 --> 00:03:20.273
<v Casey>Winter is coming, and the forecast leans warm. Environment Canada operational meteorologist Jennifer Kowal says the Cariboo returns to seasonal temperatures heading into the weekend, with highs around minus one and lows around minus eight. El Niño is expected to keep B.C. winter temperatures warmer than normal. Snowfall is much harder to call, because the pattern gives a clearer signal on temperature than on precipitation.

00:03:21.463 --> 00:03:46.935
<v Riley>Quesnel council unanimously approved an agreement with Transport Canada to replace the loader at the Quesnel Regional Airport. The city applied for full funding, and a grant of $641,300 under the Airports Capital Assistance Program was confirmed. Jeff Norburn, the city's director of community services, told council the grant covers 100 per cent of the cost, so the city's budget won't be affected.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:04:08.326
<v Casey>Quesnel artist Maureen Hobbs Wheeler unveiled a mural at Royal Canadian Legion Branch 94 on Remembrance Day. It marks the branch's centennial, coming in October 2027. Hobbs Wheeler grew up around the branch, what she calls a Legion kid, with a veteran father and years of Christmas parties in the hall. She says the mural is her way of giving something back.

00:04:08.776 --> 00:04:12.808
<v Riley>A centennial in 2027 gives the mural a second date to build around.

00:04:14.591 --> 00:04:35.423
<v Riley>Aaron Burwell is a lifelong Green Lake resident and owns Wilderness Custom Homes. Earlier this month, he and his family cleaned up an illegal dumpsite in the backcountry around Green Lake. He says the sites keep getting bigger, and he and his family ride, hunt, and snowmobile through that forest. He got fed up, and he did something about it.

00:04:36.842 --> 00:05:05.010
<v Casey>UBCIC Grand Chief Stewart Phillip says the B.C. government's plan to repeal the Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples Act will bring more uncertainty and protracted legal battles. Mandate letters released Tuesday by Premier Lorne Doerkson tasked Attorney General Caroline Elliott and Indigenous Relations and Reconciliation Minister Scott McInnis with the repeal. Phillip calls DRIPA the roadmap to reconciliation, and says it's unclear how the province would move forward without it.

00:05:06.524 --> 00:05:34.332
<v Riley>Canada and Lheidli T'enneh First Nation announced an expansion of the Upper Fraser River Salmon Conservation Hatchery in Prince George. Fisheries Minister Joanne Thompson and Chief Dolleen Logan made the announcement. The new sockeye infrastructure is valued at $10.7 million, with indoor and outdoor rearing areas and incubation equipment. The hatchery is already under construction, and the goal is to rebuild at-risk Fraser River sockeye.

00:05:34.782 --> 00:05:38.518
<v Casey>Announced, expanded, under construction. Who runs the tanks in year five?

00:05:40.126 --> 00:05:55.870
<v Casey>The Tyee reports new studies that scientists say find the Pacific Link pipeline particularly problematic for sensitive ecosystems. The Tyee's headline says the pipeline threatens salmon streams and endangered species, and the studies name 110 species at risk of extinction.

00:05:57.432 --> 00:06:22.152
<v Riley>The British magazine Country Life argues that river restoration works best when it follows the river's own processes. Leave fallen wood in the channel, where it gives fish cover and spawning habitat. Let rivers spill onto floodplains, where the water slows and sediment settles out. Narrow channels that have grown too wide. River keeper Jack Uridge writes that in many cases a messy river is a healthy one.

00:06:23.702 --> 00:06:47.214
<v Casey>France raised its bird flu risk for the mainland to high, the top of three levels, effective November 20th, according to Le Monde and other French outlets. Poultry must be kept indoors or protected from wild birds. The Agriculture Ministry cited three confirmed outbreaks in backyard flocks since late October, along with infections in wild birds and detections in 24 European countries since August 1st.

00:06:47.664 --> 00:06:52.024
<v Riley>Backyard flocks first. Officials are worried about the commercial farms next.

00:06:53.573 --> 00:07:24.931
<v Casey>A 0.06-gram transmitter tracked a male Rufous hummingbird 4,055 miles, from Lillian, Alabama, to Juneau, Alaska, in 38 days. Gizmodo and phys.org report it as the longest hummingbird migration documented to date. The bird was tagged on March 27th, 2025, and reached Juneau on May 3rd. The transmitters send Bluetooth signals that nearby phones relay to researchers, so the tracking doesn't depend on a network of stations. The study authors say Rufous and ruby-throated populations have declined in recent years.

00:07:26.371 --> 00:07:47.067
<v Riley>Nautilus reports that oceans have absorbed 35 percent of fossil-fuel carbon dioxide since 1990, and forests and other plants another 13 percent. Those two sinks are a big reason warming isn't faster. Scientists don't fully understand how they'll behave in the future, and the carbon budgets built on them carry that uncertainty.

00:07:47.517 --> 00:07:50.405
<v Casey>So half the work is done by something we can't model yet.

00:07:51.909 --> 00:08:11.357
<v Riley>A new MIT study in Nature estimates that draining peatlands worldwide has contributed about 2.7 percent of present-day human-caused warming. Peatlands are bogs, fens, and swamps that store carbon over thousands of years. Drainage for agriculture, forestry, and development has sped up over the last 200 years.

00:08:12.961 --> 00:08:34.257
<v Casey>ABC News reports the CSIRO and Bureau of Meteorology forecast that Australia's east-coast ocean temperatures could run about 2 degrees Celsius above normal over the coming months. Scientists had to extend one of the scales used to track marine heatwaves. The Great Barrier Reef could see its seventh mass coral bleaching event, and monitoring is being increased ahead of summer.

00:08:34.707 --> 00:08:39.163
<v Riley>The scale getting extended is the detail. The old top of the range ran out.

00:08:40.914 --> 00:09:07.618
<v Riley>In mid-November, at the COP31 climate talks in Antalya, Türkiye, countries were split over keeping 1.5 degrees as the central goal. Some wanted the final text to reaffirm it. Members of the Arab Group and the Like-Minded Developing Countries pushed to weaken or remove the target. The remaining carbon budget for 1.5 is estimated at 150 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide, less than four years of emissions at the current rate.

00:09:08.068 --> 00:09:11.076
<v Casey>Four years of emissions, and the argument is over the wording.

00:09:12.759 --> 00:09:34.831
<v Casey>IOM director general Amy Pope told COP31 that more than 200 million people in highly climate-vulnerable communities are at risk of displacement. She argued that helping communities adapt where they are is more humane, and cheaper, than responding after people have moved. She put the cost of adaptation at about $310 billion, against roughly $30 billion in funding so far.

00:09:35.281 --> 00:09:39.769
<v Riley>Thirty billion against three hundred and ten. That's a gap with a number on it.

00:09:41.399 --> 00:10:07.911
<v Riley>The Financial Times reports that forty large pension funds, insurers, and sovereign wealth funds warned at COP31 that climate impacts threaten financial stability. At a closed-door meeting, they said some investors remain cautious about emerging-market projects because they lack reliable ways to assess the risk. The Dutch central bank's president, Olaf Sleijpen, said the main obstacle is often missing long-term climate policy, not missing capital.

00:10:08.361 --> 00:10:12.177
<v Casey>Investors saying the policy is missing is a useful admission. Who writes it?

00:10:12.627 --> 00:10:21.859
<v Riley>Warming is loading the dice for fire and flood. Who is still on the ground to hold the land is a question we'll come back to. First, a local organization.

00:10:23.258 --> 00:10:40.762
<v Casey>Habitat for Humanity Cariboo builds affordable homes for Cariboo families in need. Its ReStore diverts building materials from the landfill, and the work funds housing projects. Giving Tuesday falls on December 1st, and if you want to support the work, you'll find Habitat for Humanity Cariboo at habitatcariboo.org.

00:10:41.439 --> 00:10:47.663
<v Riley>Keeping usable building materials out of the landfill and putting the money into homes is hard to argue with.

00:10:47.999 --> 00:10:50.775
<v Casey>Back to the backcountry, and to the burned ground.

00:10:52.175 --> 00:11:11.583
<v Riley>If warming can make fire weather about three times likelier, is the person who stays all year to replant burned Chilcotin high country protecting ground that can still be held? Or are they clinging to work the climate is about to undo? My read is that staying is how burned country comes back. Somebody has to be there with the seedlings.

00:11:12.197 --> 00:11:17.061
<v Casey>Being there isn't proof. It tells you someone is loyal. It doesn't tell you the ground can hold.

00:11:17.716 --> 00:11:22.508
<v Riley>Loyalty isn't a metric, agreed. Look at what actually went into the ground, though.

00:11:22.688 --> 00:11:23.088
<v Casey>Go on.

00:11:23.604 --> 00:12:00.762
<v Riley>My Cariboo Now reports that Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation planted endangered whitebark pine near Middle Lake after the 2023 Hell Raving Creek wildfire. That fire burned about 12,000 hectares in the West Chilcotin, and the planting sites sit at 1,800 to 2,000 metres. The land sits within Tŝideldel First Nation's traditional territory and the Eniyud Community Forest. Seedlings went in by helicopter, to remote high sites. Forestry superintendent Daniel Persson says many of the trees died, and the aim is to help the natural forest recover.

00:12:01.368 --> 00:12:23.048
<v Casey>Whitebark pine is listed as endangered in Alberta, and Canada's 2010 assessment called it imminently and severely threatened. A 2025 Canadian Journal of Forest Research paper says its populations have declined sharply, mostly from white pine blister rust, an introduced fungus. So the planting is going into a species that was already losing ground before the fire.

00:12:23.577 --> 00:12:30.849
<v Riley>Which is the case for planting. If you wait for the species to recover on its own, you're waiting on the disease to stop.

00:12:31.411 --> 00:12:49.187
<v Casey>Or on the disease to leave the range. The assessment named four human-influenced threats: blister rust, mountain pine beetle, fire exclusion and climate change. It projected blister rust alone would cut the species by more than half over 100 years. A few seedlings on one burned slope don't reach any of that.

00:12:49.865 --> 00:13:13.513
<v Riley>Nobody is claiming they do. The playbook is what matters. Identify the trees that resist rust, breed them, plant the resistant seedlings, manage beetle outbreaks, and use prescribed burning where it fits. Parks Canada says it's growing resistant trees across its seven mountain national parks. The BC government lists similar actions. A seedling is one step in a program that's already running.

00:13:14.095 --> 00:13:27.935
<v Casey>The same assessment says fire exclusion helped build the dense stands that beetles like. If the old stands were thick because fire stayed away for decades, is planting on a burned slope fixing the cause, or treating a symptom that fire already removed?

00:13:28.241 --> 00:13:36.369
<v Riley>Fire is part of the fix here, and the planting site is a burn for a reason. Prescribed burning is on the same list as the seedlings.

00:13:36.918 --> 00:13:43.622
<v Casey>Prescribed burning on a list is not the same as prescribed burning on a slope. Who sets those fires, and who signs for them?

00:13:43.925 --> 00:13:56.973
<v Riley>Forestry agencies and the nations whose territory it is. That's the governance piece, and we'll get to it. The biology is that fire opens the stand and the resistant seedlings give the species a second chance.

00:13:57.626 --> 00:14:04.474
<v Casey>Then the fire does the clearing and the seedling does the restoring. That's a lot for one burn to carry while the winter runs warm.

00:14:05.038 --> 00:14:19.526
<v Riley>The warm winter we heard about earlier matters here. Environment Canada's Jennifer Kowal says El Niño will keep B.C. temperatures warmer than normal, with snowfall much harder to call. So the seedlings go in on a planting window that's getting less predictable.

00:14:20.137 --> 00:14:30.265
<v Casey>And the snowpack that feeds those high sites is the thing nobody can call. Warm winters can mean thin snow in spring, and young trees on a burned slope don't have much root to ride that out.

00:14:30.789 --> 00:14:41.133
<v Riley>That's a risk. It's also the reason the species needs more than one season of planting. Surviving the next hot fire season is the real test, and that takes years of checking.

00:14:41.469 --> 00:14:44.317
<v Casey>Years of checking, paid by whom? We'll come back to that.

00:14:44.817 --> 00:15:13.473
<v Riley>Now the fire side. The World Weather Attribution study on Indonesia is the closest thing we have to a template. As the Japan Times reports, climate change added about 1.6 degrees Celsius of heat during the August to October fire season, and made hot, dry fire conditions about three times likelier. A very strong El Niño cut seasonal rainfall sharply. The study's own title says warming is making El Niño-driven fire weather worse across rapidly degrading peatlands and forests.

00:15:14.111 --> 00:15:22.375
<v Casey>The study puts El Niño first. Warming is the multiplier. It doesn't start the fire season, and that changes who gets blamed when the smoke comes in.

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:35.666
<v Riley>A multiplier still sets the rules for the next season. Friederike Otto, a climate scientist at Imperial College London, put it this way: El Niño turned off the taps, but fossil fuel emissions turned up the heat.

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:58.284
<v Casey>And the fuel is human-made. Nusantara Atlas, an independent monitoring platform, estimated that fires affected 2.88 million hectares from January through October. The Indonesian government hasn't confirmed that total. Its founder, David Gaveau, told the Guardian the worst fires happen on drained, dried peatland. Open burning for land clearing is the suspected main ignition source.

00:15:58.941 --> 00:16:12.373
<v Riley>That's the case for detection. Satellite monitoring and rapid attribution can tell policymakers within weeks how much warming made a disaster worse. A crew that sees the edge of a smoke column early can change the outcome.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:24.482
<v Casey>Prediction lets land-clearing interests off the hook. Knowing the fire weather is three times likelier doesn't rewire a drained bog. And in the Cariboo, who is counting the hectares that burn near Middle Lake?

00:16:24.764 --> 00:16:33.308
<v Riley>The Cariboo has its own monitoring. The question is who acts on it, which is the same question. The stayer is the one who sees the edge first.

00:16:33.654 --> 00:16:37.790
<v Casey>Sees it. Can they stop it? That's the test I'd put to any planting contract.

00:16:38.326 --> 00:16:55.206
<v Riley>Fair test. Take the part of the problem people on the land can change, which is the drainage and clearing. That MIT peatland number we heard earlier comes from drained ground. The Indonesia study's own researchers called for peatland restoration and stronger land protections.

00:16:55.895 --> 00:17:01.991
<v Casey>Ground drained on purpose. The person with the ditch machine usually isn't the one who stays through the fire season.

00:17:02.504 --> 00:17:15.368
<v Riley>That's the argument for local stewardship. The person on the ground sees the ditch before the satellite sees the smoke. Undoing a drain is a local job with a local fix, and a lot of it is about who gets to say no.

00:17:15.975 --> 00:17:30.303
<v Casey>Who gets to say no is the whole point. The Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil told Eco-Business it had received no evidence that its certified members had set fires deliberately. Nobody on the ground is testing that, and the stayers inherit the smoke either way.

00:17:30.938 --> 00:17:37.626
<v Riley>Which is why audits and local monitoring matter together. One checks the company. The other checks the land.

00:17:38.249 --> 00:17:49.145
<v Casey>And the land can't send a reply. The carbon sinks we're counting on aren't a sure thing either. If a forest stops absorbing, the budget on paper is wrong, and the burned slope gets less help than planned.

00:17:49.695 --> 00:17:56.575
<v Riley>So restoration matters more because the sinks are uncertain. Keeping forest standing is a hedge, not a luxury.

00:17:57.208 --> 00:18:03.800
<v Casey>A hedge can lose too. Restoration may be buying less than we hope, and I'd rather plan for that than find out in a bad season.

00:18:04.366 --> 00:18:12.702
<v Riley>Then measure it. Monitoring plots on burned slopes can show whether the seedlings take, and the data comes out in a few seasons, not a few decades.

00:18:13.357 --> 00:18:20.469
<v Casey>Measure them, and then fund the people who measure them, and keep funding them after the photo is taken. That's where I keep stopping.

00:18:20.969 --> 00:18:39.865
<v Riley>Staying has a human cost, though. The Guardian reports that fires, heat waves, floods and storms killed tens of thousands of people in 2026, and calls that a conservative estimate. It includes 72 dead in Brazil after February landslides, and at least 131 in Ethiopian floods in March.

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:45.870
<v Casey>Those numbers say staying can kill. Calling it virtue without a safety margin is easy from a chair.

00:18:46.415 --> 00:19:02.159
<v Riley>I'm not calling it virtue by default. Amy Pope's point at COP31 is that adapting where people live is more humane than responding after they've moved, and moving out is a form of adaptation too. Nobody should treat the person who leaves as the failure.

00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:13.080
<v Casey>The person who leaves should have a choice, not a fire line closing behind them. Some people stay because the land is the job. Some stay because the road out costs more than they've got.

00:19:13.673 --> 00:19:23.841
<v Riley>Which is the distinction the question turns on. A crew needs a written point at which a site is judged not holdable. I'd put it in the plan before the season starts.

00:19:24.458 --> 00:19:32.418
<v Casey>A pull-out rule is a start. It still needs someone other than the contractor to hold it. Otherwise the contractor is grading their own slope.

00:19:32.986 --> 00:19:38.914
<v Riley>The people on the slope should have the authority to call it. Not the office two hundred kilometres away.

00:19:39.208 --> 00:19:42.712
<v Casey>That I'll sign. If somebody writes it down, with a name next to it.

00:19:43.212 --> 00:19:58.052
<v Riley>This is why the Chilcotin is the test case. Hell Raving Creek sits on a nation's territory, inside a community forest. The planting is the visible part. The stewardship is what carries it past the helicopter, and that's a structure, not a single crew.

00:19:58.602 --> 00:20:11.578
<v Casey>So the question is whether the nation and the community forest are at the table for the decisions, not just on planting day. Who wrote the plan? Who gets the monitoring reports? Who decides whether a bare slope gets a second planting?

00:20:11.897 --> 00:20:23.017
<v Riley>I don't have the contract. The sources give us the location, the nation's territory, and the community forest. The governance terms are a question for the people who hold those rights.

00:20:23.319 --> 00:20:26.935
<v Casey>Then we can't say the nation is in charge, and we can't say it isn't.

00:20:27.533 --> 00:20:34.437
<v Riley>I'd rather name the gap than fill it with a guess. The structure is the part I'd bet on, slowly and not perfectly.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:38.572
<v Casey>Slowly's fine. I'll want to see years of checking before I call it staying.

00:20:39.207 --> 00:20:47.919
<v Riley>The Green Lake cleanup from earlier is the small version. Burwell's family cleaned up that dumpsite because they're the people who keep going back.

00:20:48.568 --> 00:20:53.936
<v Casey>Tending counts. It's also unpaid, and the spring after doesn't show who came back to check the spot.

00:20:54.535 --> 00:21:05.639
<v Riley>A family can clean a dump in a weekend. Keeping it clean takes a schedule and somebody with the authority to enforce it. Count the dumpsites next spring. Count the seedlings that survive the summer.

00:21:06.198 --> 00:21:11.094
<v Casey>Counting is cheap. Following up is not. Who does it when the crew moves on to the next contract?

00:21:11.421 --> 00:21:16.541
<v Riley>I'd like the answer to be a named person with a budget line, not a volunteer roster.

00:21:16.861 --> 00:21:20.525
<v Casey>I'll take the named person. The budget line I'll believe when I see it.

00:21:21.206 --> 00:21:33.318
<v Riley>So what counts as proof? The seedlings surviving the next hot fire season, or the crew still being there after the contract ends? I'd say both. The trees tell you about the ground. The crew tells you about the people.

00:21:33.895 --> 00:21:38.639
<v Casey>And who gets to call a burned site holdable? That's still open, and I'm not fine with that.

00:21:39.149 --> 00:21:46.653
<v Riley>It's open. If you have a story tip or want to argue back, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Enjoy your weekend.

00:21:46.927 --> 00:21:48.399
<v Casey>Pack a blanket for the back roads.

00:21:48.998 --> 00:22:08.078
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.